{#
	Preload the hero background image — it's the LCP (Largest Contentful Paint)
	element, so getting it fetching early reduces perceived load time.
	`as="image"` tells the browser to assign image fetch priority instead of
	the default "low" priority a background-image would otherwise receive.

	Two MEDIA-SCOPED preloads so each viewport preloads only the crop it will
	actually paint, matching the CSS hero swap in main.css:
	  • desktop (>768px)  → landscape crop hero-bg-veil-srgb.webp (the base rule)
	  • mobile  (≤768px)  → portrait "west" crop hero-bg-veil-west-srgb.webp,
	    which the `@media (max-width: 768px)` rule paints via !important.
	Before this split, EVERY device preloaded the desktop crop and mobile
	clients then downloaded the portrait crop the CSS actually renders — a
	wasted full-image fetch on the most bandwidth-constrained visitors. These
	use complementary range queries at the CSS rule's exact 768px boundary,
	including fractional viewport widths, so the preload selects the same crop.
	`media` on <link rel=preload> and range syntax are both
	honored by all evergreen browsers. The mobile crop already exists (generated
	alongside the desktop srgb webp on 2026-04-22); no new asset was needed.
#}
<link rel="preload" as="image" href="/assets/img/hero-bg-veil-srgb.webp" type="image/webp" media="(width > 768px)" />
<link rel="preload" as="image" href="/assets/img/hero-bg-veil-west-srgb.webp" type="image/webp" media="(width <= 768px)" />
